  "articleSummary": "**Summary of \"No Fate But What We Bill\"**\n\nThis is an analytical security-argument essay (with a Terminator-movie framing device running throughout: the robot's \"absolutely will not stop\" endurance becomes \"absolutely will not stop billing\"). The core thesis: the agentic AI threat isn't hype, but it won't arrive via a single breakthrough — instead, two ordinary compounding curves multiply: (1) agent *endurance* (METR's task-length doubling every ~4–7 months) and (2) *breadth* (orchestrators coordinating dozens of sub-agents, evidenced by Anthropic's GTG-1002 disclosure where Claude Code orchestrated espionage across ~30 targets). Threat capability scales as duration × breadth, while security review scales only linearly. A key economic point: \"LLMjacking\" (Sysdig's term) — attackers using stolen cloud credentials means victims pay the compute bill, breaking the industry's assumption that the capability user is the payer, so price/access gates filter buyers but not tenants. Finally, swarm economics make per-attempt failure rates irrelevant: cheap automated attempts (1 − (1−p)^n math) mean the swarm only has to be right once. Intended audience: engineering/security teams skeptical that agentic AI risk is overhyped; tone is analytical but punchy and rhetorical, using recurring metaphors of Skynet-as-distributed-system, \"the robot is an API call,\" and billing/meter economics.",
